    transcript: `Coordinator: Good morning, Riverside Community Garden. Ben speaking. How can I help?

Maya: Hello. My name is Maya. I moved to the neighbourhood recently, and I saw your volunteers working last weekend. I would love to join, but I have never grown anything before.

Coordinator: That is absolutely fine. Several of our members started without any experience. We pair new people with someone who has been here longer, so you will always have somebody to ask. First, do you know where the entrance is?

Maya: I walked past a gate on Market Road. Is that the one?

Coordinator: That gate belongs to the school. Our postal address is on Cedar Road. That is Cedar, spelled C E D A R. The garden is between the school playing field and the canal, but there is no entrance from the canal path.

Maya: Thanks. And is there a membership fee?

Coordinator: Yes. Adult membership costs twenty-four pounds for the whole year. We used to charge thirty pounds, but a local business now helps us buy seeds. Children can come with an adult at no extra cost. Your membership includes all our regular gardening sessions.

Maya: That sounds reasonable. I work during the week, so I would need to come at the weekend.

Coordinator: The session for new volunteers takes place every Saturday. Our experienced team also meets on Wednesday evenings, but beginners should attend the weekend introduction first. It starts at ten and usually finishes by midday. You can stay longer if you like, although there is no obligation.

Maya: Where should I meet the group when I arrive?

Coordinator: Please wait by the east gate. The main gate is used for deliveries, and it can get quite busy. There will be a volunteer wearing a green jacket to welcome you. If you cycle, there are racks just outside that entrance.

Maya: I was wondering what happens in bad weather. Do you cancel the introduction?

Coordinator: Only if travelling would be unsafe. If it is simply raining, the group meets in the greenhouse. We can practise sowing seeds there and look at the planting plan. The shed is too small for a group, and the classroom at the school is not available at weekends.

Maya: Do I need to buy any equipment before I come?

Coordinator: We provide tools, including small ones for people who find heavy tools difficult to use. Please bring your own gloves. They should fit comfortably and protect your hands. Wear shoes you do not mind getting muddy, and bring some drinking water if the day is warm.

Maya: What are you planning to teach this week?

Coordinator: The first practical activity is making compost. We will look at which kitchen scraps can go into a compost bin and why dry leaves are useful. Planting vegetables comes later in the month. We want everyone to understand how we keep the soil healthy before we start planting.

Maya: That would be useful at home as well. How do I book a place?

Coordinator: Send us an email with your name and the date you would like to attend. Please use email rather than the online form, because that form is being repaired. You can find our address on the noticeboard at the garden entrance. We will reply to confirm your place.

Maya: Is there somewhere nearby to find out more about gardening while I wait?

Coordinator: The library has a small display of books chosen by our volunteers. The bookshop has gardening books too, of course, but the library display is specifically for beginners. It is beside the information desk.

Maya: One last thing. My nephew is sixteen. Could he come on his own?

Coordinator: Yes. Sixteen is the minimum age for volunteering without an accompanying adult. Younger visitors are welcome, but an adult must stay with them throughout the session.

Maya: Wonderful. I will send the email this evening. Thank you for your help.

Coordinator: You are very welcome. We look forward to meeting you.`,
